<h2>A supply chain built backwards</h2>
<p>The case began on 9 April 2026, when the Narcotics Cell intercepted one Vansh Kumar Gautam near Budhpur Service Road with 160 grams of smack. A day later, Majid Ali @ Tunnu was arrested with 302 grams recovered from his residence — a commercial quantity. The petitioner was arrested on 17 April 2026, not on any recovery, but on Majid Ali&rsquo;s disclosure naming him as the supplier. The chargesheet followed on 6 June 2026. The Special Judge (NDPS), Rohini Courts, dismissed his bail application on 5 August 2026, and he moved the High Court.</p>

<h2>What the prosecution had — and what it did not</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>What the State relied on</strong><br>Majid Ali&rsquo;s disclosure statement naming the petitioner as his supplier, and regular WhatsApp contact between the two numbers.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>What was missing</strong><br>No contraband recovered from the petitioner; no phone recovered; the attributed number registered to a third person; no CDRs or financial transactions linking him to the co-accused.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Parity</strong><br>Co-accused Prempal had regular bail from the trial court since 5 June 2026, and Vansh had been granted bail by the High Court on 17 November 2026.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody and trial stage</strong><br>Arrested 17 April 2026; charges not yet framed; a long trial ahead.</div>
</div>

<h2>Section 37 applied, not avoided</h2>
<p>Because the recovery from Majid Ali was of commercial quantity and conspiracy under Section 29 was alleged, the rigours of Section 37 NDPS Act were in play. The Court worked within them rather than around them. Relying on <strong>Mohd. Muslim @ Hussain v. State (NCT of Delhi)</strong>, (2023) 18 SCC 166, it applied the prima facie standard: the court does not weigh evidence as at trial, but asks whether, on a plain reading of the record, there are reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty. It also drew on <strong>Vinay Dua v. State Govt. of NCT of Delhi</strong>, 2025 SCC OnLine Del 4534, where bail followed from the absence of corroboration of a co-accused&rsquo;s disclosure.</p>
<p>On the facts, the Court noted that <em>&ldquo;no contraband has been recovered from the petitioner&rdquo;</em>, and that as to the WhatsApp chats and calls, <em>&ldquo;it is not clear whether they were in relation to the supply of contraband.&rdquo;</em> With the prosecution itself attributing a lesser role to the petitioner than to Vansh — who was already on bail — the Court concluded that it <em>&ldquo;finds reasonable grounds for believing that the petitioner is not guilty of the alleged offence&rdquo;</em> and that he was unlikely to offend while on bail.</p>

<h2>The order and its conditions</h2>
<p>Bail was granted on a personal bond of Rs. 25,000 with one surety in the like amount, to the satisfaction of the trial court. The conditions follow the now-standard Delhi template: residence intimation to the investigating officer, the mobile number furnished to the Jail Superintendent and kept switched on, no tampering or inducement to witnesses, joining the investigation when required, and appearance on every trial date. The Court recorded, as always, that its observations were confined to the bail application and expressed no view on the merits.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters in practice</h2>
<p>NDPS prosecutions regularly extend liability up the supply chain on disclosure statements alone. This order is a clean illustration of the materials a bail court expects before Section 37 hardens into a wall: recovery from or at the instance of the accused, communications whose content is actually established, or a financial trail. Where all three are absent, the commercial-quantity recovery from a co-accused does not, by itself, keep an alleged supplier in custody indefinitely — particularly where charge has not been framed and co-accused with comparable or greater roles are already out.</p>
<div class="note">Bail findings under Section 37 NDPS Act are prima facie assessments for the purpose of the application alone. They do not decide guilt, and the trial proceeds unaffected. Case details are as recorded in the judgment dated 23 November 2026.</div>
